labor wrapped up its conference in  Liverpool today and it's clear the party  has a spring in its step after a  well-received leader speech yesterday  and strong poll leads and all that  before more financial turbulence today  kirstama has called for the immediate  recall of parliament over the economic  crisis well we can speak now to the  Shady Shadow attorney general Emily at  Thornbury who joins us now thank you  very much for being on the program today  at Miss Thornbury I'm not sure if you  caught the whole of the interview with  Chris philp the main takeaway for me was  when I asked him if they were going to  change course he said no we're not  what'd you make of it  I know the complacency is just  extraordinary everybody else in the  country all commentators around the  world are unanimous in saying they have  made An Almighty mistake with our  economy  and they're just gonna blunder on what  we need is we need the government to  come back to Parliament it's the  appropriate place for them to come and  to reassure the markets that they have a  plan for for funding these tax cuts I  have to say why we're going through all  of this why are they trashing the  economy for the sake of one percent of  people getting more money on the back of  unfunded tax cards I don't know but in  any event they've spooked the markets  because of the way that they have been  talking it wasn't just what they did it  was also what they uh briefed they  briefed that they were going to go  further I thought it was interesting  that Chris Phelps didn't seem to be  talking about cutting the sorry bringing  in the personal allowance for higher  rate taxpayers which was something they  were briefing so perhaps they've gone  back on that but they're still talking  about they're not going to tell us how  they're going to pay for all this stuff  until the 23rd of November that and what  is going to happen in the meantime the  markets need reassuring what we're going  to see I think is quasi quartet you're  swanking onto the stage in Birmingham  for the Party Conference and telling us  all that everything is fine and the any  problems that there are are not his  fault and that he's doing the right  thing and I have 2 000 of the Tory  faithful telling him that he's right  whereas the rest of us will be holding  our heads in our hands and thinking what  are they doing there's one thing that I  don't understand right because Labor's  made a big deal of being the  economically credible uh party the party  of you know sound money of economic  discipline at your conference you were  talking there about unfunded tax cuts  fiscally irresponsible tax cuts but  labor would keep the 1p cut the basic  rate of income tax and you say that you  would reinstate the 45p rate but then  you would immediately spend that money  so that's not being fiscally responsible  either is it because you do exactly the  same thing in fiscal terms  so I think it's there's a number of  things let me just give me a moment to  answer it so the first thing is is that  unfortunately we fear that we're not I  mean hopefully we'll be in government in  two years time but it's two years and so  you the the 19 Pence tax rate will be  embedded by that stage into our our  fiscal system and frankly we haven't  gone into a single election and since  including 1997 saying that we were going  to raise the basic rate of tax that  isn't what we do it isn't what we're  about we're not about increasing the tax  burden on working people so it will have  been worked through by that stage the  question is really is how do they deal  with the emergency now you know the  emergency of their Creation with them  saying we're going to do this we're  going to do that we're going to do this  and it's all going to be on the back of  yet more borrowing a hint we may do more  hint we may start cutting public  services and this is just not the way  that you increase growth if you're going  to increase  borrowing you do it in order to properly  invest which doesn't mean a sugar rush  of giving tax cuts to the richest it  means doing things like we've said which  is setting up a Great British Energy  company that will be publicly owned and  they will increase the amount of energy  that we create within our own country so  that we're not dependent on Putin so  that we're able to get to Net Zero  faster and so we're able to push down  the energy bills I mean that's how you  ought to spend money that's what you  should be doing that's a kind of real  plan not what they're up to which is  just looking after their mates so given  that you say that you know the levels of  borrowing are uncomfortable given that  you say that labor doesn't want to go  into an election promising  um tax Rises for working people are you  also going to go into the next election  promising to spend more money on health  and education given there's no money no  more money left  so there's a number of things that we  have said already we've said as a  principle that we need to have a fair  attack system that we have at the moment  and we've begun to show the way in which  we would go so we would not be cutting  corporation tax for example  um and if they there wasn't the cut the  corporation tax then that would be 18  billion we would have a windfall on  those on those energy companies that at  the moment are making enormous profits  they were never expecting to make those  profits but on the back of what's  happening internationally prices have  gone up so much that they're making  whacking great profits and we've said  well we all have a windfall tax on them  the government for reasons which are  Beyond understanding are saying they're  not prepared to to tax these they have  insurance  they have introduced one window yeah  yeah but but since then the prices  continue to go up and will continue to  go up so their profits will continue to  go up and the person in charge of BP for  example says that it's like having a  cash machine you yeah when he's when  it's put to him which is one of the Tory  defenses which is oh well these  companies will stop investing if we tax  them too hard he says no no no we  continue investing okay so all of this  is nonsense we would change the non-dom  status and the rules in relation to that  that would make us uh three billion so  there's a series of things so we would  be doing differently to the government  and of course I mean the one thing I  agree with Christopher on is he says  every pound should count well done right  right I do until you squeeze one more  question in if I may because you and I  both been in Liverpool at Labor  conference I don't know if you agree  with me but it felt to me like the most  buoyant frankly labor conference that I  can remember in quite a long time and  there's been numerous polls of course  showing a rather healthy uh poll lead  for the labor party do you think you're  going to win the next election  yes I know be fine  I can't say yes or no because I don't  want to say anything for granted and I  know that here isn't either we really  want to have the opportunity to serve  and we will be asking the British public  in two years time okay thank you very  much indeed Emily Thornbury there for  the labor party
 

